In article <93271.101739U21187@uicvm.uic.edu>, 
John Schulien <U21187@uicvm.uic.edu> writes:

> What is the storage capacity of an RK05, RK05J, and an RK05F?

The RK05 and RK05J are the same capacity.  Assuming the
PDP-11 version:

	Removable (single) platter
	2 heads (tracks)
	12 sectors/track
	200 cylinders (plus 3 "spares" -- never used as far as I know,
	but a great place to stash info you don't want anyone to find.)

For a grand total of... 4800 blocks!!!

The RK05F has a non-removable disk.   It is the same cartridge
as the RK05J, but is fixed in place.   The RK05F emulates two
RK05J drives.

> Also, what is the density of a TU10, and is it 7 or 9 track?

800 bpi only.   7 or 9, depending on the head/electronics installed.
Very few 7-track units were built, but one of my customers still
uses one.

> I've got a chance to get some of these free. Are they worth the
> effort required to lug them home?

No.   I have two RK05F drives.   In a few weeks I will remove
all the parts from the *heavy* aluminum baseplates and melt the
baseplates into something useful.   (Yes, I do metal casting
and machining.)

> Are they repairable by mortals (like me?)

Yes, I have done it many times.

