alan@itwol.bhp.com.au (Alan Wilkie) writes:

>I know this response came from Digital, so I guess they have the final
>word on this sort of thing, but I thought that the 4000/90 was a 32 VUP
>machine.  If I'm not mistaken, a VAX 11/780 is 1 SPECmark, the same as
>VUPs, so it follows that 32 SPECmarks is approximately equal to 32 VUPs
>(allowing for the differences that cache sizes, etc, will have on
>different benchmarks).

Another case of making the logical (but invalid) extension of VUP to SPECmark.
The SPECmark of a VAX 780 is, by definition, 1 SPECmark IF IT IS RUNNING THE
SPECIFIED VERSION OF THE OS AND COMPILER! A 780 using the current C compiler is
faster than 1 SPECmark. A similar statement can be made about VUPS except that
they are always "normalized" back to the reference 780. This is possible since
VUPS only compare machines of the same architecture while SPEC is not so
limited.

Bottom line is that most VAX systems will tend to have slightly higher
SPECmarks than they have VUPS. And, as compilers improve ad systems speed up,
the divergence will be greater. Benchmarking is a tricky business, even on a
single architecture, let alone across RISC, CISC, and whatever else.

There are three kinds of lies: lies, damn lies, and benchmarks. -- apologies
to Mark Twain.

