>The bog-standard RK05 (also known, I think, as RK05J)
>is a 2.5Mb front-loading cartridge drive. The RK05F seems to be
>a sort of semi-winchester with a capacity of 5Mb. Both are 19"
>rack-mount, and *heavy* (about 110 lb). Things to watch out for:
>1) make sure you get the controller (RK11 -- backplane + four boards);

Not necessarily. That's an RK11-D. The RK11-C was a 10.5" unit with about 100
flip-chip boards and a separate H720E or H720F power unit.

>2) make sure you get some cartridges;
>2) keep the heads clean (I find that surgical spirit seems to
>do the job; isopropyl alcohol is specified in the manual, which
>may or may not be the same thing);

Use 91% isopropyl alcohol, 9% distilled water.

>3) keep the cartridges in polythene bags when they're not in
>use;

Recomended by DEC, but by no means essential. I've used cartridges that I found
in the skip, after blowing the dust off.

>4) replace the filters (Inmac used to sell them, and presumably you
>can still get them from DEC);

They last a lot longer than the 6 months specified by DEC!. They're expensive -
about 100 pounds a time as well.


>5) if you're transporting the drives, lock the heads (if only 
>to protect the nicads -- see below). There's
>a small metal bracket that you unbolt and rotate to prevent
>the positioner carriage from moving. Viewing the drive
>from the front, with the top cover off, it should be bolted to the 
>upper right-hand side of the front positioner frame. Unbolt it, 
>turn it round so that it presses against the front of the 
>carriage, bolt it back down. If you get given the set of 
>transit brackets (which protect the baseplate shock-mounts), 
>use them too.

Yes. That's important. Never trust the NiCd pack to retract the heads. You
don't need to clamp the baseplate though.


> 
>Note also that there is a set of nicad batteries which are
>intended to retract the heads if the power fails. Allow a few minutes
>after powering up for these to charge before spinning the drive
>up, just in case. If they're old, and the drive is used 
>comparatively rarely, there's a danger that they won't 
>retain enough charge from the last session to get the 
>heads fully home.
> 
>The controller is cabled to the drive using standard BC11 Unibus cable,
>and the drive-bus is terminated using a standard Unibus terminator
>module.
> 
>The biggest problem I've had with these drives is the extensive
>use of foam rubber to seal the internal airways. Over time, the foam
