: In the days when all terminals could be traced to a physical location
: by following the wires from the bulkhead of the DH (look-alike) to the
: terminal, such a program would have been useful (or at least cute).
: A have a vague recollection that the PDP 11/70 running UNIX at the
: University of Oklahoma had such a program.  Once one showed up there
: were undoubtly many "one-up" versions.  Who knows, David Cathey may
: have even worked on one...

 I once wrote such a utility for CCSU's pair of 8650's.  It was voluntary
 to use tho, as you had to enter your location at login. We had a group
 set up called 'VAXTOOLS' and it was part of the VAXTLOGIN.COM procedure
 to ask for your location.  Subscribers could then use an enhanced
 SHOW USERS command to get locations and pithy quotes from a pseudo
 .PLAN file.


: Today, hardwired terminals are much less common, having been replaced
: by terminal servers.  Just from the name of the terminal (/dev/ttyxx
: on UNIX or LTAxx: on VMS) you can't tell where it may be.  If you can
: get the terminal server name and port number, then you should be able
: to track them down, if the connections are stable.

