 alan@itwol.bhp.com.au (Alan Wilkie) writes:
> In article <27t2ud$odo@usenet.pa.dec.com>,
  rjones@pa.dec.com (Russ Jones) writes:
>> The Model 90 was characterized broadly as 32.7 SPECmarks ('89 variety), 
>> 34 TPS, 24 VUPS.
> 
> I know this response came from Digital, so I guess they have the final
> word on this sort of thing, but I thought that the 4000/90 was a 32 VUP
> machine.  If I'm not mistaken, a VAX 11/780 is 1 SPECmark, the same as
> VUPs, so it follows that 32 SPECmarks is approximately equal to 32 VUPs
> (allowing for the differences that cache sizes, etc, will have on
> different benchmarks).

Try 24 VUPs, or 42MVUPs.
                ^^Looks like a digit slipped on that 32.

32.8 SPECmark89
26.7 SPECint89
37.5 SPECfp89
12.91 Linpack single MFLOPS
7.27 Linpack double MFLOPS
57.35 DR Labs CPU2
  [From DECDirect Workgroup Solutions Catalog, Winter/1993, CGY]

If I'm correct, SPECmarks are a benchmark measure of performance based on some
standardized algorithm.  Compare: sieve of Eratosthenes.  The performance of
a standardized algorithm is not necessarily *directly* representative of
machine performance as measured by some other criteria, such as VUPs.
Therefore, the correlation is most likely not as straightforward as applying
a simple scaling factor.

Additionally, due to measurement precision, and the tendancy to express
1.08 or 1.33 as simply '1'... even if a simple scaling factor were applicable,
you probably wouldn't have sufficient resolution at 1 VUP or 1 SPECmark to
accurately compute such a ratio.
