whd0675@cs.rit.edu (Walter H Dick, III) writes:

>I have found that Exabyte tape drives are not that reliable with video grade
                      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ 
>tapes.  The purpose of a backup is to retrieve a copy in an emergency.  Saving
 ^^^^^
>a few dollars and using video grade is playing with fire, IMHO.  I use Maxell
>D8 tapes.  They work quite well and are not that expensive.  Also, I have found
>that Exabytes are bit flacky at times.  I have 8200s, 8200-SXs, and one 
>8500 on site.  Everyonce in a while, they will get stuck.  You will need to
>recycle the power so that it will release your tape.  I have a couple of DAT
>drives around.  I kind of prefer them, for the following reasons:


    I can not emphasize my agreement with this statement enough. Video
grade tapes are NOT going to last long enough for long-term archives
(which can mean as little as one year under poor temperature and/or
humidity conditions), they ARE going to lose metal particles, and with
them your data, and with the loss of those particles, will require you
to clean your drive more often, which will in turn tend to wear out
sooner.
    You might also reflect on people who stand to lose money, jobs (like
the system manager's), or valuable time if the backup is worthless.
Large parts of the scientific data from the $50M (at the lowest
estimate) SMM spacecraft were archived on video grade Exabyte tapes. It was
not a good idea, I can say in retrospect, though in our own defense, we
can plead that there was nothing else available in '88. Now there is,
and anyone who fails to look seriously at alternatives has only themself
[is that a word?] to blame.
    Question: why did Exabyte, sole owner of 8 mm data technology, buy a
DAT manufacturer last year? And: why has no 4 mm vendor tried to license
8 mm technology from Exabyte?
    My $0.02.
						Joe Gurman

