We have also had some bad experiences with video grade tapes with Exabyte
drives running at 2GB capacity. We now use data grade tapes, usually
with the Exabyte brand. These are readily available here, so we have
not tried other brands to see how they perform.

>> I use Maxell D8 tapes.  They work quite well and are not that expensive.

As mentioned above, we have not tried this brand of tape. However, I have
read an article in a magazine called Studio Sound (a very recent issue) that
compares the quality of different tapes by looking at their error rates.
Note that they performed these tests as there has been some discussion of
the use of DAT tapes for use as studio master tapes. Long life and 
reliability has been an issue, as there have been some problems experienced
in this area. Anyway, the Maxell DAT tape that was tested was one of the
better performers.

>     Question: why did Exabyte, sole owner of 8 mm data technology, buy a
> DAT manufacturer last year? 

This sounds a lot like market pressures to me. DAT drives are much more 
compact, and the personal computer market is a lot bigger than the
market for minicomputers and workstations. Their approach makes good
