In article <1993Sep28.090553.1@fencer.cis.dsto.gov.au>,
st_waldman@fencer.cis.dsto.gov.au wrote:

[various stuff deleted]
> 
> As mentioned above, we have not tried this brand of tape. However, I have
> read an article in a magazine called Studio Sound (a very recent issue) that
> compares the quality of different tapes by looking at their error rates.
> Note that they performed these tests as there has been some discussion of
> the use of DAT tapes for use as studio master tapes. Long life and 
> reliability has been an issue, as there have been some problems experienced
> in this area. Anyway, the Maxell DAT tape that was tested was one of the
> better performers.

    Anecdotally, our use has also shown Maxell DAT tapes to be the best of
{Sony  video 8 mm, Maxel video 8 mm, Exabyte video 8 mm, HP DAT, and Maxell
DAT}. But I doubt (1) that we're close to the real bit error rate (that
would take ~ 10^3 tapes, even for 8 mm), or that (2) Studio Sound used data
grade tapes. In the case of DAT, I'm certain of it, because the small holes
in the fron uderside of the DAT cartridge let the DAT drive know what kind
of cassette it is, and in the U.S. (we're tools of our Japanese-owned
recording industry), unrecorded 60- and 90-meter tapes wil be rejected by
"audio" DAT drives (as opposed to DDS drives).
> 
> >     Question: why did Exabyte, sole owner of 8 mm data technology, buy a
> > DAT manufacturer last year? 
> 
> This sounds a lot like market pressures to me. DAT drives are much more 
> compact, and the personal computer market is a lot bigger than the
> market for minicomputers and workstations. Their approach makes good
> business sense, irrespective of the merits of either system.

    Er, probably good logic, but Exabyte now sells a half-height version of
the 8 mm drive, and most PC's still have 5.25-inch bays, so I'm not certain
good logic applies to reality (as usual).
> 
> >And: why has no 4 mm vendor tried to license 8 mm technology from Exabyte?
> 
> Same as the above. There is no point tackling what is effectively a 
> monopoly market that is a lot smaller than the DAT market (IMHO).

    Once again, perhaps this is true worldwide, and includingaudio DAT
outside the U.S. (where 4mm audio is a dead letter), but this year is the
first when DDS drive sales outpaced Exabyte drive sales in the U.S. (By
'95, they're predicted to be 2-3 times as high --- that's why Exabyte
"diversified.") And they wouldn't have been tackling Exabyte: Exabyte has
licensed its technology to a Japanese manufacturer (the name escapes me
now) --- ironically, since the mechanism originated with Sony, to whom I
believe Exabyte still pays royalties --- and a year or two ago, would have
been happy to see other U.S. manufacturers license it as well. But the
handwriting was already on the wall. The Exabyte drives are so many
albatroses around the necks of small computer system managers.
    We had a good laugh today when we opened the case of an Exabyte drive
here that was replaced by service personnel after about a year of ragged
service --- it turned out that was nearly 4 years ago! Sometimes the '89
drive works, sometimes it doesn't. Is it because of 4 years of missed ROM
upgrades, or more basic problems? Dunno. Our 1990 DAT drive, though it only
takes 60-meter tapes, has never been replaced, and chunks away happily to
this day. Anecdotal, but just another straw on the camel's back. Can't wait
'til we copy our last 8 mm cassette to DAT (the unreliability of the
Exabyte drives, even a 6-month old 8500, slows us down), so we can throw
the 8mm drives down the stairs. For six years, they've been flakey enough
to be major, continuing headaches. I wish they;d been just a bit worse: we
could have given them up altogether years ago.
> 
> >     My $0.02.
> 

> Just my AUD$0.013 worth. (the present exchange rate is not too good here)
> 
    Another US$0.02. And speaking of $, 2/4 Gbyte compressing DATs are
getting cheap enough here (and will probably drop even further with the
introduction of DDS-2 drives this year) that I'm beginning to dream of one
for the home machine. Even if Exabytes were of as good quality, the drives
cost 60% more for a negligible difference in capacity.
    And I though we'd have optical tape by 1993....
