[Hmmm. Time to get your news software fixed, I see...]
In article <22SEP199310025323@vx.cis.umn.edu>, tkennedy                         writes:
> We have been running dual (or perhaps duel is more correct) 11/70 systems, and
> as I am now out of hair from the constant hardware failures, we have decided
> to chuck them for a MicroVax or some such. Unfortunately, we have been in RSX-
> land so long that we know zilch about what to change to or how to do it. I
> appeal to you all for direction.

  I find 11/70 systems to be incredibly reliable when properly maintained.
Unfortunately, that means self-maintenance, as neither DEC nor 3rd-party
service vendors care to work on them any more. As an example, one 11/70 here
at SPC has 132,000 power-on hours and has had less than 3 hours of downtime
since November 1988, when it was donated. And this is a "classic" 11/70 in
H960's, MK11 memory, Massbus disks and tapes.
 
  If you are experiencing frequent CPU problems, I'd check the power supply
voltages and the card cage fans, for starters.

> Our peak load is 26 users, each in tasks of about 50 KW, accessing a single
> disk. At any moment, about half of the tasks are stopped, waiting for input.
> The 11/70s handle this poorly even when they are running. What machine
> do I need to properly handle the load? Will I have to rewrite and recompile all 
> my Fortran 4 programs? Should I quit and sell encyclopedias?

  In what way does the system "poorly handle" tasks waiting for input? Slow
echo? Dropped characters? Other?

  Anyway, yes, if you move to a VAX (or any other non-PDP-11 system) you will
have to recompile your applications. Depending on your use of OS-specific
features, you may have a lot of rewriting to do as well. VMS supplies many of
the features found in RSX, sometimes with subtle (or major) differences. DEC
has a RSX emulation package which will run many RSX executables unchanged
under VMS. However, the last VAX with hardware compatibility mode was the 
8650, so the emulator's performance on MicroVAXen is likely to be quite poor.

  I know of a number of sites that looked at converting, but when they looked
at the amount of system-specific stuff, decided to stay with the 11/70's. One
of them spent over $2M in a conversion before giving up. On the other hand,
some conversions are nearly painless.

  To get a better idea of what's involved, pester your salescreature for the
two books "RSX and VMS Compatibility Guide" (Part number EJ-31489-41) and
"FORTRAN Compatibility Guide" (P/N EJ-31491-41).

  Also, you will need more CPU power than you'd expect. I know lots of folks
who thought they could move from an 11/44 to a MicroVAX II because the MVII
was "faster". Most of them are now on MV3900's.

