In article <27qf0o$k4b@slab.mtholyoke.edu>, mcrowley@mtholyoke.edu (Michael A. Crowley) writes:
> When a place like Midwest Systems or the Newman Group advertises
> new equipment at low prices, are there any catches or problems
> with the equipment?  Any things that one might be careful about
> buying this way?  More specifically with things like terminal servers,
> repeaters, bridges, terminals....

  It's sort of like airline tickets - no two people pay the same price. DEC
has become less flexible on their pricing lately, and in an effort to reduce
prices to "commodity" levels, they wound up increasing prices for some cus-
tomers like me. Oh well...

  Anyway, different vendors mean different things by "new". I stopped dealing
with Midwest a long time ago, for a number of reasons. At the time, Midwest's
"new" meant something like "hasn't been used for long by someone who lost all
the manuals, accessories and packing materials".

  The things you need to consider are:

  1) As above, what is meant by "new"? Are all the items that would be in the
     package from DEC present, such as manuals, accessories, etc?

  2) Is the item up to the current revision levels?

  3) If the item requires software, is a license included? Will you have to pay
     DEC a license transfer fee? Will you have to buy the software and documen-
     tation kits from DEC?

  4) What are the warranty terms?

  Depending on what you're looking at, you can save a lot of money in the 3rd-
party marketplace, especially if you have one of an item already and thus don't
really need the manuals, etc. When you buy from DEC at the more expensive 
price, you get on-site service for the item. At DEC's lower price, you get a
return-to-factory warranty. Both of these are normally for a year. Note that
vendors who aren't "Digital authorized resellers" or somesuch can't pass a DEC
warranty along to you, so even though the item is "new", DEC may not honor the
warranty. Even so, you might be able to save, since you can often buy 3rd-
party and pay for a year of DEC's service and still come out way ahead of the
game.

