    I hesitate to disagree with Terry (hmm, having two t kennedy's
 in the same thread is going to be confusing) but some years ago I
 had to move code from RSX-11 on 11/70s to VAXes and it wasn't all
 that bad.

    However, a lot depends on how the original code was written.

    I second the suggestion to the the compatibility guide from
 Digital.  It will help a lot.

    If your Fortran code is in fact 'pure' or nearly pure Fortran,
 it should move to the VAX with little or no change.  If you are
 calling system services, then you may need some changes.  Some
 things, like QIOs, operate the same on VMS as on RSX: you just
 have to change the order of some parameters.  Some calls, like
 getting system time, change names: but there is an equivalent on
 VMS to nearly everything on RSX.  VMS will have many nice
 features that aren't available on RSX, and when you move you may
 want to take advantage of them.  But I moved a lot of programs
 with no changes at all, just a recompilation.

    And you will be happy to know you can throw out all of your
 task builder option files.  Overlays cease to exist.

    If you have other specific problems or questions, I'm sure one
 of us has run into it before.

    Bart Z. Lederman


