We have 3 Alphas here running OSF/1 1.2, and I've noticed that they seem to
have a problem background-mounting directories over nfs. For example, one 
machine has in its /etc/fstab the lines:
/home/formentor@formentor       /home/formentor nfs rw,bg 0 0 
/home/llucalcari@llucalcari     /home/llucalcari nfs rw,bg 0 0
which seem OK to me, and in fact usually do mount the requested directories perfectly. The problem comes when we have a power outage, which happens quite
frequently (maybe once a week) around here. Then all the machines reboot, 
including the alphas, but whereas the other machines here (Suns, HPs, Nexts)
crossmount each other properly when they come up, the alphas usually don't
manage this. Typically two of the three alphas will be missing some of the nfs
mounted directories, which I have then to mount manually with mount -vat nfs, 
at which point everything is OK. So, the question is, what's different about
nfs on the alphas that makes this happen. I've already tried increasing the
number of mount retries from the default of 10 000 to 100 000, but this didn't
make any difference to the problem - I didn't expect it would, since 10 000
should be plenty. It seems to me that the alphas can't actually be trying
10 000 times. Has anyone else experienced this problem, and even better, cured
it? (Can you reply to me as well as the newsgroup, since the connection here 
is a bit unreliable - thanks)
