In article <1993Sep23.191639.2073@govonca.gov.on.ca>, loxtonr@govonca.gov.on.ca (Ron Loxton) writes:
> Netters:
>
> 	I am attempting to perform task-to-task communication between a
> Vax running
> Pathworks V4.1 and my PC over decnet. I can send data from the VAX task
> to my PC task
> but not the other way (I get a symbolic stack dump).

It is impossible to provide any suggestions with this limited information.

How about posting:

	- a very short, COMPLETE example program that demonstrates the problem
	- a copy of the stack dump from that program
	- at the very least, the ERROR MESSAGE from the stack dump!

Heck, for that matter, you didn't even mention which machine the stack dump
happened on.  (I assume it was the VAX, since PCs usually just lock up under
unanticipated error conditions.)

However... if you're using $QIO[W] to read and write the DECnet link... I can
tell you that the ONLY difference between the read and write calls will be that
the function code changes from IO$_READVBLK to IO$_WRITEVBLK.

I can also tell you that the most common cause of a stack dump is probably an
access violation.  In fact it will say "access violation" near the top of the
display.  The usual cause of an accvio is that you tried to dereference a
pointer that "pointed" to non-existent virtual address space.

