In article <CDruAz.E6t@fc.hp.com>, chan@fc.hp.com (Channing Benson) writes...
> 
> [...]  Unix command-line pipes are arguably a perfect example of
>peer to peer client-server computing (albeit, in the absence of rsh,
>restricted to a single host).

No.  Client-server computing requires an ability to include multiple hosts. 
What your talking about is merely mutiltasking extended via rsh to have some
minimal client-server ability.

> 
>You missed the point. It has nothing to do with what commands are available,
>but how those commands can be combined to do things unimagined by their
>creators.
> 
>Designers of client-server systems would do well to create architectures
>with the flexibility and creative power of Unix pipes.
> 

Bull.  Having done client-server, UNIX, and VMS for many years I feel strongly
what the designers need is the power of VMScluster technology.  Does for us
what we have to do by hand in the UNIX environment.

Pipes are cute and powerfull, I and haven't the foggiest idea why DEC hasn't
listened to the many requestes to put them into DCL, but client-server
computing needs a lot more, such as aynchronous signalling across multiple
cooperating platforms and data base access locking.  Without non-pipe
technologies such as NFS, we'd have even more work to do ourselves in UNIX
based client-server applications.

