>Tim Whibley/CERN Geneva, Switzerland

Yeah, but that DLT2000 is absolute overkill for someone who's got "2
Gigabytes maximum to back up."  Also, does the DLT2000 really use TK70
or TK50 tapes?  The TZ85 and TZ86 definitely do not -- they require a
TK85 (CompacTape III), which costs in the neighborhood of $40 US.

The DEC TZ85/TZ86 family are excellent backup drives.  We consistently
get 700 to 800 Kbytes/sec to tape with them.  They are substantially
more reliable than helical scan formats (i.e. Exabyte), and not as hard
on the media.  You should really only use an Exabyte-type tape about 10 
times before you quit relying on it.  The CompacTapes don't have that
limitation. 

Where I've found the TZ85/TZ86 drives really shine, though is when
you're restoring.  Don't ever forget to take that part of the equation
into account when you're shopping for a drive (after all, why are you
doing backups? :-)).   Based on the seek time data supplied by the
vendors, we determined that it would take > 8 hours longer to restore
one of our 1.5GB drives with an Exabyte drive than with the DEC drive
(worst case, of course, restoring 1 image backup and 19 incrementals
from tapes with multiple save sets on them).

Granted, as another poster mentioned, these DEC drives are not very
common, so if you're expecting to write tapes on one of these drives and
take them to other systems, you're more than likely to be disappointed.
8mm and 9-track tapes seem to be the most popular for transport between
different systems.  

But if you want fast, reliable backups, and considerably faster restores, 
than other technologies offer, check the DEC drives out.  The tapes are 
more expensive, but they don't have to be considered disposable, so you 
may not spend that much more in the long run.  DEC also sells the drives 
to other vendors, so you aren't stuck with dealing with DEC if you don't 
want to.

