I'm not sure what to tell you.  Under DECnet/OSI for VMS V5.6, it's a LOT
better than it was under DECnet/VAX Extensions 5.4.  The manuals are much,
much, much, MUCH better, to start with.  

There is nonetheless a lot of new terminology, new concepts, etc., to learn.

I find NCL to be largely "opaque" -- it is very difficult to get around in it
unless you already know what you're after.  A quick example:  If you know the
name of a class of entities, you can quickly find the names (and all other
attributes) of all entities of that class on your machine.  BUT THERE IS NO
EASY WAY to find out what entity *classes* exist on your machine.  NCL HELP
will tell you about ALL of the entity classes that may exist, but there's
no way to find out which ones DO exist except by going through all of the
possibilities shown by HELP.  

A workstation with a couple of 48-line (at least) DECterms, one for browsing
NCL HELP and the other for actually doing NCL, is a must.  Also plan to keep
several square feet of desk space free, for the various manuals that will all
have to be open at once.  Plan on using lots of Post-Its, and keep GOOD notes.

The worst thing about it, from my perspective, is that there is a TERRIBLE
amount of manual configuration that has to be done.  Ex.: Let's say you want to
use FTAM to access files stored on node BIGDISK.  Well, surprise:  To FTAM, the
node names you defined through DNS don't mean a thing. You need an "alias
name", which is a name that translates to something "the RMS presentation mode
of the FTAM responder on the node with NSAP xxxxxxxx, using connectionless
routing."  (There are actually one or two other elements to that -- I don't
have my references handy.) You define these names in a special database.  The
entries in the database can't refer to the node names defined through DNS,
either; full NSAP addresses (lots and lots of hex characters) go in there. 

I've yet to hear a good explanation of what all this grunge is supposed to do
for us.  

