This is exactly what happened when DEC tried to set up one DNS server with
an attached DEMSB (X.25 gateway). It took them quite a long time to get it
all running, though the secondary server still doesn't work and the two
nodes using DNS are unable to reach any other node (including the server).
The trouble is that most DEC people seem to belong exclusively to one of two
categories: those who know Phase V, and those who know Unix.

[ ... ]

> I've yet to hear a good explanation of what all this grunge is supposed to do
> for us.  

I don't mind converting to modern, "future oriented" protocols, but our first
mistake was to assume that it would be easy to migrate from Phase IV to
Phase V; to me it seems as easy as to migrate from UUCP to Novell NetWare :-).
The second mistake is to assume any gain in functionality before the turn
of the century; while Phase IV under Ultrix is a solution for no obvious problem
when you compare it to IP, Phase V seems to reside more on the problem
side ... :-).
Stealing the words from an X400 debate: "Phase V is the protocol of the
future - lets keep it that way" :-)


