In article <275r34$gsv@news.delphi.com>, dcongdon@news.delphi.com (DCONGDON@DELPHI.COM) writes:
>I've got a question for all the RSX-11M+ gurus out there.  I bought
>a PDP-11/23 about nine months ago which had RSX-11M+ 3.0 loaded but
>no backup tapes.  I have a TK25 (DC600A QIC drive) on the machine and
>wish to backup my system so I can recover if I blow away the system
>as I learn RSX-11.  Two questions:
> 
>1.  How do I backup the whole system onto tape?
> 
>2.  Can I make some kind of bootable tape which will let me reload
>    the OS from my backup tape (assuming I render the system totally
>    inoperable)?                                

Don, the best and safest way is to use BRUSYS, a minimal RSX which allows
to init and backup disks and tapes.

Start BRUSYS by typing boo[6(or1),54)brusys

The mini RSX will come up and asks you for date and time.
AFter typing 'RUN BRU' the system asks you for the first (input) and
second (output) device. If your system uses nonstandard CSR addresses and
vectors type '/DEV' in response to the first question, the system shows you a
list of available devices.

At the BRU-prompt type in the input device and output device and thats it.
(BRU>/VER DU0: MS0:) This command saves disk DU0: to tape MS0: in a way that
after restoring the disk from tape the system is again bootable from the disk.
If you spoil your disk badly you will have to boot the system from tape. 
The only thing that you still need now is a BRUSYS or BRU64k system on a tape,
but  I can't remember how to proceed creating such a tape. It must be somewhere
in the SYSTEM MANAGEMENT GUIDE, perhaps another kind soul remembers the
commands ...

hope this helps

Martin

