As another reply has already explained, the standalone BRU will produce a 
bootable system image on tape.

To produce a bootable tape containing the standalone BRU system, there is the 
the VMR (Virtual Monitor Routine) utility in RSX. Don't have a RSX system
management manaual at hand, but I think the command is 
  RUN $VMR
then, at the VMR> prompt, enter 
   LB:[3,54]BRUSYS/IM      or wherever the system image of brusys resides
  and SAV ddn:label
where ddn: is the device specification of the tape (MU0: ?)

And be aware to use a fresh tape: VME SAV will initalize the tape!

Such a tape can then be hardware booted (provided Your CPU has a bootstrap ROM
for the TK25), and bring up BRUSYS, which in turn can restore the backup of the
system disk.

Good luck! (I'm sorry to have no more RSX system in my hands since 4 years!)

