bg@tnis.frmug.fr.net (Bernard.Guillaumot) writes:


# Who has informations, elements of studies, advantages-disadvantages
# keypoints and/or miscellaneous experiences with the LAN solutions
# NOVELL 3.11 in comparizon with PATHWORKS (VAX/VMS enviromment -
# Extented LAN network with routers) ?



Well, here's a real show-stopper.  There are several flavors of
Pathworks _Server_ s/w: VMS, OS2, Ultrix (OSF?), and soon to be
Windows NT.  VMS is by far the most popular.

VMS has an architectural limitation that prevents the BACKUP program
from performing _incremental_ BACKUPs/RESTOREs of files to directories
that are deeper than 8 descendant subdirectories.  It is possible
for an _image_ backup or an _image_ restore to get all data on
a volume, regardless of subdirectory depth.

A client PC or Mac accessing a VMS Pathworks file server may create
files/folders/directories that are much deeper than the 8 levels,
and never receive an error message, or know that their files are in
jeopardy of not being backed up.  It's as easy as drag & drop; a
user can drag a folder to their network drive, without knowing that
the folder they're copying has folders that are too deep.

So given this background, let's say you have 200 users sharing a
3.5 Gigabyte disk.  You perform incremental backups Monday through
Thursdays, and a full (image) backup on Fridays.  Your disk crashes
on Thursday.  On VMS, you would first restore your last full image
backup (from last Friday), then restore your incremental backups
in reverse chronological order.

But if any PC/Mac files had been created below 8 folders deep between
Monday through Thursday, they would not be restored.  Period.
Same implications if a user accidentally deleted a file/folder
on Thursday.  If that folder/file was below 8 subdirectories, and
was created/modified between Monday-Thursday, they're SOL.  Files
and/or folders below 8 levels are only saved on image backups;
incremental backups can't see below 8 levels.

In case your wondering, I have tested this by creating folders at least 
21 levels deep on a VMS Pathworks server.  No error message given.  There 
is absolutely *NO WAY* of controlling this, even under the current
versions of Pathworks for Mac (V1.2) or DOS (4.2-1).
