In article <1993Sep18.173748.1@alien.gici.com>, laut@alien.gici.com writes:
> 	Is there any department within DEC where you can "haggle" for
> discontinued products?  I'm seriously getting into computer graphics
> (especially raytracing) and need _tons_ of CPU horsepower, but don't have
> tooooo much cash to pay for it.  So, I was curious if there is a group
> within DEC that I can call, to offer them 'X' dollars for fifteen 
> DECStation-5000/240s with 24mb memory each, thinwire Ethernet, and nothing
> else; and then see if they bite at it.

  Your best bet would be the B1 and B2 product categories. These are un-
refurbished and refurbished equipment, respectively. To get a price on an
item, you can use DEC's E-store and give the part number with a B2 prefix.
For example, a RA90 would become B2-RA90?-??. There are occasionally some
good deals here, although many times a used equipment broker can give you
a better price, especially since DEC doesn't discount the B2 stuff. Your
best deals will be when DEC packages licenses with the systems.

  If you're looking for a small number of systems and can qualify as an
ISV (Independent Software Vendor), DEC will give you discounts of up to
46% (last I looked). I don't have contact info for the ISV program, but
calling any main DEC office number (603-884-5111 comes to mind) and asking
about the ISV program should work.

  DEC has a "Best For Less" program which is almost always limited to sub-
assemblies and spare parts. They're at 800-225-5385 and an [outdated] list
can be found on the E-store under "special programs and offers".

  Your local DEC rep (provided you have one and have a working communi-
cation channel with him/her/it) is empowered to cut deals. It can't hurt
to ask.

  Again, brokers/resellers come to mind. Look in the DEC-specific trade
magazines, magazines like "The Processor", etc. My favorite one is a com-
pany called Compunet International. They are small enough to really care
about their customers, can get just about anything you need, and have
really good prices. Call Tom Loonan at 800-767-9779.

  DEC used to have giant end-of-quarter and end-of-fiscal-year blowouts
which major resellers were invited to. These were big secrets and DEC would
never admit to them. They were designed to move product (often at a big
loss) in order to show good "numbers" for the quarter or year. I remember
one where a lot of over 500 LA120-DA's went for $250 each (and this was
when these were new and selling for nearly $2000). I don't think DEC has
done this for 7 or 8 years, though, and it's unlikely you would be buying
enough to interest them even if they were.

