|>>UNIX system administration is *infinitely* better to deal with
|>than VMS.

This seems rather a glib statement to me. You have to remember that
the Unix
style of system admin is different to VMS. In Unix, admin is,
typically,
performed by editing some file somewhere. You have to know which
file and
you have to know the syntax for the file contents. I'm not aware of
any
standardisation here. Of course, the advantage is that you can use
your 
favourite editor (vi, emacs, ed?!?). The disadvantage, is that there
are no
checks on the validity of the contents of such files when it is
updated.

In Open VMS, most admin is done by running a specific utility that
manipulates a database. The advantage here is that the utility can do some
validity checks before the database gets updated, so you can be sure that
the result is OK, at least to the system. The disadvantage is that the
system manager has to learn the command syntax of several utilities to
acheive anthing. Of course, these utilities nearly all come with a
comprehensive built-in help facility.

Personally, I prefer the VMS style, as I think it is more user friendly
and safer. (What happens if you get the contents of the Unix file wrong?).
But then that is what I am used to. If I had spent the last ten years doing
only Unix admin, I might think differently.

BTW, my main experience is VMS, but I have run Sun and HP systems.

Tony Arnold,
OpenVMS Systems Manager
University of Manchester, UK.


