In article <1993Sep20.124723.3437@tigger.jvnc.net>, dataram@tigger.jvnc.net (Dataram Corp.) writes:
>
>The last DEC unix OS I used was ULTRIX 4.0.  Can some one tell
>me if adding SCSI disks is about the same for DEC OSF/1 as
>for ULTRIX?  For example, can I expect to have to

About the same with the differences noted below.

>
>1.  Use MAKEDEV in /dev directory to create special device
>files for block and charcter transfer.

The same mostly.  MAKEDEV takes care of it.

>
>2.  Add the disk type to /etc/disktab

Still optional.

>
>3.  Use chpt (or something similar to write the partition
>table to the disk

The partition table is now part of a disk label.  You create
and modify a disk label using the disklabel(8) command.  If
you use a disk-type not in /etc/disktab, disklabel(8) will
use the default partition table, DEVGETGEOM to get the geometry
and assorted other defaults.  You can also edit the disklabel
with disklabel(8).  I think it uses EDITOR to decide what editor
to run.  Examples; first create a disklabel for a known disk,
then create a disklabel for an unknown disk, then edit the
label:

# disklabel -wr /dev/rrz1c rz26

# disklabel -wr /dev/rrz5c dontknow

# disklabel -e /dev/rrz5c

See the manual page for more information.

>
>4.  Use newfs to create the file systems on the disk.

Unlike ULTRIX V4 and later, newfs(8) will use the information
from the driver and label if no disk-type argument is given.
Under ULTRIX you had to give the device name again.

>
>Are there any limitations on disk size for DEC OSF/1?  As
>I recall ULTRIX 4.0 was limited to less than 2 GB for a
>while.

There are always limits.  However, with the switch to 64 bit
numbers for off_t I think the device limit based on the magnitude
of off_t went up to about one or two terabytes.  As of the last
SPD that I saw, the largest supported devices were the RA73 and
RZ73, both 2 GB.  I've used an RZ74 (3.5 GB) for a few minutes
and it worked as I expected it to.  I suspect the RZ74 and RZ28
are being qualified.

Since OSF/1 supports LVM the largest volume I've heard supported
is either 40 GB or 64 GB.  Check the SPD, which may be somewhere
on gatekeeper.dec.com.

>

One difference you didn't ask about and worth noting is that
the device numbering in the configuration file is a little
different.

The OSF "drive" numbers increment by 8 between devices:

device disk rz0 at asc0 drive 0
device disk rz1 at asc0 drive 8

Where under ULTRIX it would be:

disk rz0 at asc0 drive 0
disk rz1 at asc0 drive 1

The other difference is the leading "device".  This would be obvious
the first time you looked at the configuration file.  Current versions
of the OSF/1 configuration file builder don't fill out the possible
devices on each SCSI adapter like ULTRIX did, so you probably will
have to edit by hand.

