In article <1993Sep21.102221.25940@syma.sussex.ac.uk>, nickw@syma.sussex.ac.uk (Nick Watkins) writes:
|>My colleagues in Astronomy have a DEC 3000 400 on test and have noticed 
|>that it seems to allocate a minimum of 
|>8Mb of virtual memory to each running process !!!
|>This causes it to run out of steam rather quickly as more than one
|>user is added. The VSZ entry below is what we are basing this belief on.
|>Is this correct, and why is it doing it ? 
|>
|>Our HP 720s and 715 seem to allocate VM as needed.

which means they aren't POSIX compliant.  POSIX requires that you allocate the
memory that could be used at the time of startup.

it also means they are probably running 1.2 instead of 1.3 and are using the
default swap algorithm(eager) instead of what most vendors use(lazy).  so they
should do one thing for sure and consider the other:  1. UPGRADE TO 1.3
2. Consider using lazy swap.  See the manuals for details on the trade-offs of
using this method.

|>
|>ambitron> ps aux | more
|>USER       PID %CPU %MEM   VSZ  RSS TT  STAT STARTED       TIME COMMAND
|>root     16581  0.0  6.2 11.8M 1.87 ??  S    10:10:39   0:01.68 
