In article <26qn17$f6k@sol.TIS.COM> mjr@tis.com (Marcus J. Ranum) writes:

	   This was a prerelease kit that, while "supported" is no longer
   being maintained, has nowhere near the functionality of the OSF/1 for
   Alpha, and is nowhere near "commercial quality code." This was code that
   was published in Q1 of '92 and has had no fixes, upgrades, support,
   or tuning since then. The compilers are old, the version of X is old,
   the utilities are old, there are no layered products, etc, etc.

All true, but in its favour, I must say that I find it better than
Ultrix

	   I can appreciate Russ' wanting to cast this in a good light,
   but he's stretching things if he's REALLY recommending that anyone
   who wants OSF run the prerelease SDK. It was a one-shot and Digital
   has since abandoned it, along with the rest of Digital's OSF/1 and MIPS
   strategy.

   >As announced previously, Digital will not be expanding processor coverage 
   >to other members of the DECstation family or supporting later revs of 
   >DEC OSF/1 on DECstations.  There are upgrade programs to assist customers 
   >that want to migrate to Alpha AXP and DEC OSF/1 when the time is right.  
   >Talk to your local Digital sales representative for details.

	   What this means is:

	   "It's old."
	   "It's abandoned."
	   "We'll sell it to you as a hook to get you to migrate to Alpha."

We have DECstations here running the MIPS OSF/1 SDK.  We're expecting
to have to spend *at least* another 4000 pounds per machine just so that
we can get a machine that will run an up-to-date version of OSF/1.

Needless to say, we weren't budgeting to purchase 60 SPECmark machines
at over twice the price of our original machines when we made the decision
to buy DECstations.  Now we have hardly any of our original grant left
and we're stuck with an unsupported operating system.
