From: tillman@swdev.si.com (Brian Tillman)
> 
>>Comments with regard to comparing "real Unix" to some arbitrary 
>>"Open-X", a Posix compliant version of a proprietary OS.  Not 
>>intended as comment with regards to any particular OS.
>> 
>Why do you and so many other people seem to want to deceive people into 
>thinking that Unix is not proprietary?  HP-UX is every bit as 
>proprietary as, say OS/MVS or OpenVMS VAX.

As was pointed out, contemporary usage has often provided truthful
contexts for word usage which expand older English definitions.
Sometimes the expansions are analogues to earlier usage. Sometimes
they're puns.  Sometimes they're apparently even contradictory. But
once ubiquitous, they're never deceptive except accidentally with
respect to the very naive.

The de-facto truthful definition of a proprietary OS is "you can't buy 
essentially the same OS from several competitive hardware vendors". 
MS-DOS, for example, despite being completely owned, controlled,
and sold for profit by Microsoft, can be truthfully described as the 
quintessential non-proprietary OS.  A more sophisticated, more
precise statement of current usage would note a connection to the 
amount of license fee charged by the OS owner (Novell in the case 
of Unix).

That's just the way the terms are commonly used.

Dan Hepner


