In article <272ot2$ip4@TAMUTS.TAMU.EDU> andrewd@vincent2.tamu.edu (Andrew Duchowski) writes:
>I originally thought that the 3100 could share /usr together with
>the 5000.  Barring the various /var pointer re-linking, this almost
>worked w/out a hitch, except for the X window not coming up with
>the login prompt on the 3100.  I found that the 3100 can share
>everything in /usr (except /usr/sys/MIPS/<client-name> which keeps the
>kernel config stuff in there) except /usr/bin !  I don't know which X
>files are screwing up (and why).  Anyway, what I did was keep two 
>copies of /usr/bin, one for each server and client.  I know this is a 
>terrible waste of space -- do any of you know which X programs are at
>fault here?  Is it Xprompter, dxsession, Xws or maybe /usr/bin/login
>that don't seem compatible (all these have something to do with the
>:0 entry in /etc/ttys, which leads me to believe they're all responsible
>for keeping the X window manager and login prompts going)?  Is it
>perhaps Xws that gets confused if one tried to "run it twice" (although
>on different processors)?  This kinda doesn't make sense though since
>I'd think that two distinct processes are invoked...

It is likely Xws - the X servers are specific to the graphics hardware
that you have.  If the 5000 has any of the PX or PXG series hardware, you
need a different X server.  The one you need is dependent upon the hardware
that you have.

If you check the /etc/ttys file on each machine, you should find a line -
usually the last one in the file - that is responsible for the Xprompter
starting up on the given system.  Part of that line contains the name of
the X server.  I suspect that in one /etc/ttys you'll find a reference to
/usr/bin/Xws, and the other will have a reference to something else.  If so,
copy the executable in question to the shared /usr/bin.

Also, make sure that you end up with separate /var for the systems.

If it's any consolation to you, this definitely CAN be done - I've done it
any number of times.  I'm not sure what Digital's official posistion on
support of the configuration will be, though.

