Interesting.  Someone *actually* thinking in terms of throughput instead
of speed.  Very refreshing... 

Now, don't get me wrong, I'm *quite* happy with the Alpha on my desk --
generally speaking.  It is extremely fast, but under high load
situations quite often provides very poor throughput for (relatively
short) periods of time (X server freezes, etc... eventually comes back).
 I haven't fully diagnosed why, and I gather some problems have been
fixed in OSF/1 1.3 (I'm running 1.2).  The alpha is, for various
reasons, our main development box, and, although it works very well for
a single developer, quickly goes south when multiple developers start
beating on it.  Beefing it up will probably help some, but it isn't
clear to me that just getting the fastest box, for some defn of
"fastest", is always (ever) the answer.  

The WEITEK folks seem to have actually thought about their situation and
picked a solution that would get their work done quickest, which quite
often (at least in my experience) is not the case (esp. in academia,
where we are often working with whatever machines are given to us via
grants or what have you -- sort of ironic, eh?).  The brief description
given in Mr. Plamondon's article sort of smacks, philosophically, of the
SPRITE system's approach (better utilization of shared resources vs.
blind addition of horsepower that might not get utilized properly
anyway)...  if only this were the dominant attitude in the industry as a
whole.  I wonder how many spare cycles the internet has at any given
time; has anyone ever attempted to find out?  It would be interesting. 

Random thoughts over coffee.  Hope it isn't too much a waste of bandwidth. 

