In article <26irk2$qce@TAMUTS.TAMU.EDU> mrz9517@TAMUTS.TAMU.EDU (Michael Raynold Zika) writes:
>    We're looking into adding some "crunch" power to our network -- basically
>a system that is dedicated to crunching through large jobs -- and would like
>some other opinions.
>[...]
>  We would like a system that can handle everything from large memory jobs
>(100+Mb) to small memory jobs (~3Mb) possibly running a mix at the same time,
>possibly running a single job at a time.  Good I/O is a necessity -- some
>of these codes write 100Mb files each run.  Upgradability is also important --
>as the jobs grow, so must the RAM size (and CPU speed if possible).

At WEITEK, we're trying out an unusual approach to this: a stack of SPARCstation
II boxes used as compute servers.  The goal here is to execute large numbers
of simulations and other circuit-design-related jobs simultaneously.  Using
a stack of inexpensive CPUs has advantages in solving the "run many jobs
quickly" arena:

* You avoid disk I/O bottlenecks, since each computer server has its own
  disk for /usr, /tmp, and swap.

* SPARCstation 2s are standard, cheap (as refurbished or clone units),
  interchangeable, and easily repaired.

* One workstation crashing has little impact on overall productivity.

* The performance of the individual systems is fine, once you replace
  the original processor with SPARC POWER uP.

* Various pieces of software already exist for load-balancing across
  machines on the network.

While the performance of a SPARCstation II may not look like much compared
to a newer, high-end system, the high-end systems are not immune to the
performance problems caused by running multiple large jobs, especially when
you start hitting swap-speed bottlenecks, which are more disk- than CPU-
oriented.

In terms of dollars per SPECint (or SPECfp), the use of existing systems,
clones, or refurbished systems is pretty attractive -- providing your
problems are oriented toward multiple independent jobs (which probably
applies to X terminals, multi-simulation environment, and many other
environment where it's typical that many users need more CPU power than
they have on their desktops).

	-- Robert

