I missed the beginning of this thread, but I feel forced to make a small
comment...

At any point in time there will always be one or more systems that are
significantly faster/more powerful than the others.  Currently, since
Alpha is the newest major architecture, it is (probably) Alpha.  It has
also been Sparc, HP's architecture, MIPS, etc. etc. at various times.  If
all you care about is the most bang for the buck right now then you probably
can't go wrong by simply buying the fastest machine right now.

However, if you're going to be using this machine for more than a year or so,
then you should seriously consider several other things, including the future
of the architecture (including scalability), support (both from the vendor and
from software companies - for example you should ask your major supplier(s) of
software which system _they_ use to develop their software before porting it
to all their supported platforms - if you use the same platform you'll get
higher quality code and you'll get it faster), history of the company,and
size and stability of the company.

I'm not saying that any one architecture is better than another, and I'm
not saying "don't buy DEC".  I'm just giving some advice...  (the fact
that we're stuck with 100 DECstations that we've been buying for the
last three years, when there is no real future for them, is beside the
point :-(   ).

Lezz


