In article <26q09t$igk@email.tuwien.ac.at>, ulrich@mips.complang.tuwien.ac.at (Ulrich Neumerkel) writes:

>
>I would like to avoid that certain processes that use a lot of
>virtual memory deteriorate all other running processes on the machine
>by using "their" real memory.

ULTRIX (nor BSD I suspect) have a mechanism to limit the working
set.  If the paging system is tuned reasonably, inactive pages
should be paged out and the virtual size of a process doesn't
matter (except in its use of page/swap space).

There may be some commentary on the choice of using global paging
in the 4.3BSD design book; "The Design and Implementation of the
4.3BSD UNIX Operating System".  The number of pages that desire to
be active is greater than the number of pages avaialble, you'll
have to page.  The only other choice is reduce the number of
pages wishing to be active.  I doubt setting working set limits
will do that.  It will simply cause one application to page
more so that another may page less.  I suspect the total amount
of paging is the about the same.

