It could be "stiction". This is a common problem in the older Seagate MFM
drives. I have a drive that has had this problem for 3 years. The best solution
is don't turn off the computer once it's up. To get my drive spinning after it
has been off for a few days, I use a small screw driver to spin a part of the 
spindle that is exposed on the outside of my drive. Once the drive is 
"unstuck", it will spin up normally. I don't remember what the RD53 looks
like. Some drives may not have access to an area that will allow you to
spin the platters.
