Pretty much the "other way around".  The 8500 was a "bounded" (limited
expansion, smaller cabinet, cheaper) version of the 8700 which had been
deliberately slowed down.  (Don't ask me why we did this - I never understood
and I know it annoyed a lot of Digital engineers.  I think there was some
sort of concept of a uniform price/performance figure.  As far as I know,
we don't do this sort of thing anymore.)  After a while, the 8550 was
introduced as an 8500 replacement, with the "healthy" Nautilus processor
running at full speed.

