> To me, this isn't quite the same as what happened to the 8500, where the
> processor was deliberately modified to run slower.  I don't remember if it
> was a microcode change, or what.  Actually, this practice used to be very

The 8500/8530 to 8550 upgrade was a "real" upgrade -- it involved pulling
the backplane and much of the CPU.  (I think there are something like 11
boards on a Nautilus CPU, plus memory & BI cage.)  8500 to 8530 was a micro-
code change; apparently it just removed NOPs from the ucode.  Since the
Nautilus loads its microcode from the console subsystem, the "upgrade" came
on a disk.

My impression was that the 8500 "hobbling" came in the middle of the 
develpment cycle, with the CPU working at the rated 6 VUPS, giving time to
actually build a 4.5 VUPS machine (the 8530) a little cheaper and with
lower spec parts than the 6 VUPS version.  Marketing struck again by 
requiring a 3 VUPS version, the 8500, to live between the 8200 & 8700, hence
the NOPped microcode.

