One well-known manufacturer of IBM-compatible mainframes sold
multi-million dollar computers with a dip switch-selectable model
number. Flick the switch, change the nameplate, and charge hundreds of
thousands of dollars. (Actually, to be fair, some systems didn't come
equipped with this feature from the factory.) This feature was
sometimes offered to give customers "variable performance" on demand.
  Another maker of non-IBM-compatible mainframes, my former employer,
sold both "performance impaired" and "memory impaired" systems. The
low-end system had a long delay after each instruction, the mid-range
system had a short delay after each instruction, and the next model up
the scale had no delay.  Upgrading involved replacing one little
circuit board and possibly a bit of "tuning" and running diagnostics
under voltage and frequency margins, followed by a new name plate, and
presenting the customer with a large invoice.
  Their trick for memory was also cute. The low-end system could be
purchased for a lower price with "less memory". In fact, all the
memory was installed, but some of the address bits were "screwed
with". The memory upgrade involved replacing a few pieces of
twisted-pair, and I think one little circuit board, and the result was
more memory. The really funny thing was that this "feature" could also
be enabled by a toggle switch and "pre-installed" when the salesman
had a good idea that the customer would want the "upgrade" in the
future. Customers were always so happy when we upgraded the memory
during a PM shift. Little did they know...

