Speaking purely from personal experience over the last 6 months or so, I find the 
VMS FORTRAN on the Alpha to be a joy. The speed is more or less comparable to the
fastest IBM RISCs (er, provided you don't do any I/O) but I don't want to get
into a discussion of benchmarks. Being able to use the VMS debugger is also
a great benefit -- finally I have a workstation fast enough that I don't have to
fight with DBX, yeh!

Codes will blindly recompile but you should probably use the flag 
/assume = "dummy_aliases" to get the right answer (if you HAVE dummy aliases
(you probably do) and you use the default /assume = "no_dummy_aliases" 
then you get REALLY tricky bugs to debug, especially if you use complex numbers!!)

The FORTRAN has a whole ton of new stuff that I haven't tried yet, the most interesting
to me, however, seems to NOT work. You can now open files with CONVERT="little_endian"
or CONVERT="big_endian" or whatever you want to read in UNFORMATTED binary files from
