No, I hadn't, nor can I find any information on it.  The most recent reference
I can find is dated June of last year, talking about it as an existing
system.

However, I'll risk a guess and say that this is a system that uses a MIPS
processor run at a lower clock speed.  This sort of thing is very common
in the industry given the variable yields on microprocessors.  As the chips
come off the fabrication line, they are tested and "binned" to separate
those that are good only up to speed X from those which can run at a faster
speed Y.  The Y chips go into your more expensive models which use a faster
clock, the X chips (which you may have more of, depending on how your
yields work out), go into the cheaper models.

To me, this isn't quite the same as what happened to the 8500, where the
processor was deliberately modified to run slower.  I don't remember if it
was a microcode change, or what.  Actually, this practice used to be very
common - one well-known manufacturer of line printers basically made one
model, but had jumpers on a board to select the speed, and they'd sell the
different speed models at different prices.  Nowadays with price-performance
the driving goal, it would be ridiculous for a vendor to spend extra money
to slow down a product.  Sure, you might make one with a smaller cache, but
that's not the same as putting a "speed limiter" on the processor.

