In article <CCuCnr.6or@acsu.buffalo.edu>, scole@acsu.buffalo.edu (Steven M Cole) writes:
> Well, it seems as if in the 'VAX_8300' thread, pretty much every 8xxx has
> been mentioned, except for this beastie.. A friend is looking for some
> basic specs on the machine, CPU, Bus arch, VUP's, power requirements, 
> history, really anything notable.. 

The 8600 and 8650 really shouldn't have been given "8000-series" numbers. They
used an SBI bus (13.3 MB/sec on a good day) just like the 780.  In fact the
8600 was originally going to be called the 790.  You can still see evidence of
this in the file names in the SYS$LOADABLE_IMAGES directory. 

I don't know their "VUPs ratings" for certain.  I believe that the 8600 was 
about 4 VUPs, and the 8650 not quite half again that.  

These machines were pipelined, and used custom ECL gate arrays.  The type of 
power was just like all "big VAXes" -- nothing really used anything but 120V,
but you needed several legs' worth of 120 to meet the total current needs, so 
three-phase was what was spec'd and was really the only practical way to go.  

Besides the use of the SBI from the 780, their kinship to the rest of the
VAX-11/7nn series, lies in the fact that they implemented the complete VAX
instruction set -- including compatibility mode.  I believe they were the last
members of the VAX family to do so.  Certainly they were the fastest.  

(As someone said soon after the MicroVAX I, which lacked compatibility mode,
was announced:  "I'll never be able to look my Data General-using friends in
the face again.  They were right!  You can't trust anybody with a mode bit.")

