In article <1993Sep5.224347.2669@cmkrnl.com>, jeh@cmkrnl.com writes:
> I don't know their "VUPs ratings" for certain.  I believe that the 8600 was 
> about 4 VUPs, and the 8650 not quite half again that.  

  There are two versions of the clock module for the 86x0 - one has a fixed
set of values (different for the 8600 and 8650, of course) and the other was
fully programmable in (at least) 1Mhz steps. With this card, you could get
any performance level you wanted (within reason) as long as you didn't mind
the occasional machine check 8-)

> These machines were pipelined, and used custom ECL gate arrays.  The type of 
> power was just like all "big VAXes" -- nothing really used anything but 120V,
> but you needed several legs' worth of 120 to meet the total current needs, so 
> three-phase was what was spec'd and was really the only practical way to go.  

  Actually, the blower in the 86x0 is a 3-phase device. With the blower off,
the system will shut down within about 10 minutes with an airflow fault. The
power supplies may be 3-phase as well - I'd have to look at the print set.
However, since you won't get far without the blower, it's kind of moot.

  Despite the idiotic plug that DEC put on the system (a story in itself: DEC
wanted to crack the IBM market, so they put this giant Russell-Stoll 60A plug
on the system because it matched a very common outlet in IBM shops), the sys-
tem takes less than 15A per phase. I measured it at about 14.8/11/12.5. My
8650's use adapters which have the giant 86x0-style receptacle and a 30A plug
(same as on the 78x).

> Besides the use of the SBI from the 780, their kinship to the rest of the
> VAX-11/7nn series, lies in the fact that they implemented the complete VAX
> instruction set -- including compatibility mode.  I believe they were the last
> members of the VAX family to do so.  Certainly they were the fastest.  

  There was also some parts commonality - they used the DW780 board set (the
Unibus adapter), the CI780 board set (CI adapter), etc. In fact, with the
appropriate cables you could hook up a 78x's SBI expansion box to the 86x0.

  The 86x0 was done by the Large Systems Group, while most other VAXen were
from Midrange Systems or Micro Systems. This group tended to look at the fast-
est system around, go away for 5 years, and come out with an amazing machine
that was 5 times faster. Unfortunately, they were very expensive systems and
not well understood. The only VAX systems by the Large Systems Group were the
86x0 and the 9000. They did put amazing debugging features in them, though -
when an 86x0 is ill, it will save just about every major signal line's state
to the RL02 for later diagnosis. About 40% of the CPU modules had diagnostics
that would tell you exactly what component was defective, based on the snap-
shot file. The rest of the diagnostics would just call out the board. Work on
expanding the coverage to the rest of the diagnostics was halted when it be-
came clear that there wouldn't be a follow-on CPU using the same core design.

