In article <1993Sep8.092621.1@uwovax.uwo.ca>, brent@uwovax.uwo.ca (Brent Sterner) writes:
>
>	Russ, thanks.  Yes, it helps.  One follow-on query though.
>	If this fs is so good, is there any reason *not* to use it?
>	(Supplemental: if not, why isn't it bundled and standard?)  b.

Bundling an entire new file system into the system takes time to
do the integration work.  A slip on the part of the group doing
the file system slips the schedule of the base product.  A slip
of the base product has the file system group being idle while
they wait for their product to go out the door, or they wait for
their base functionality to appear at customer sites so they can
start selling the layered product tools.

It is a general software engineering problem of making major changes
to a software system.  It is often desirable to stage the introduction
of something big that could adversely affect the rest of the system
if the integration isn't perfect.

One problem with it not being bundled is that you can't put /usr
on it and still be able to do upgrades.  With tight integration
an OS upgrade gets all the pieces, including the various file 
systems.  It has to for the upgrade to work.  Until the tight
integration is there, interesting parts of the file system could
become unavailable until the upgrade is complete and you can't
complete the upgrade until those file systems are available.

For something like the POLYCENTER Advanced File System you could
expect the long term integration to go like:

Bundle the license, layer the software.

Bundle the software as an option.

Wait...  This wait gives customers the opportunity to learn
how to manage it.

Make the software the default subsystem.

You can try to compress the steps, but if anything breaks
along the way, you may have to many side affects.
 
