>supporters wrote back?)

Ok.

Comments with regard to comparing "real Unix" to some arbitrary 
"Open-X", a Posix compliant version of a proprietary OS.  Not 
intended as comment with regards to any particular OS.

Summary: Unix is more than Posix.

1. Process utilization with Unix is significantly different than most
   proprietary OSs.  In particular, a process is created for every 
   program run.  Process creation is relatively cheap on Unix.  When 
   making a choice, benchmark fork(2) and exec(2) on the OSs, and 
   compare.  If you expect to use your Posix compliant system as a 
   Unix system, you'll be creating and destroying a whole lot of 
   processes.

   The more general point here is that "Posix compliant" does
   not infer "usable if you use Posix".  All it guarantees is
   that compliant software will link, run, and (maybe eventually) 
   get the right answer.  Other things to benchmark include
   the CPU cycle cost of using raw devices.

2. TCP-IP comes with most Unix at no additional charge.  Having
   a Posix compliant system without TCP-IP (and all the associated
   commands and interfaces) is like being intelligent, but speaking 
   and reading only Finnish.  

3. Serious software (e.g. RDBMS) is typically very performance driven, 
   and typically utilizes some Posix compliant system calls, and some
   OS hooks which are similar among various Unix vendors.  The
   promise of an "Open System" is the freedom to buy both hardware
   and software from competing vendors.  Compare both your current and 
   future options should you decide you need to change either hardware 
   or software vendors.

4. Compare the price.  The last thing you would like to do is to
   buy a "slow, expensive Unix" while rejecting a "fast, cheap 
   Unix".


On the other side--

As others have pointed out, not everyone wants to convert from a
proprietary world to Unix.  Many wish to exploit their experience
with a proprietary system. If one's requirement is simply to have
Posix compliance available with no intention of actually using it,
then the decision is obvious.

Dan Hepner
Not a statement of the Hewlett Packard Company.


