In article <26km00$7r2@TAMUTS.TAMU.EDU>, andrewd@sematech.tamu.edu (Andrew Duchowski) writes:

>I have a Decstation 3100 and a Decstation 5000 that I'd like to hook up 
>together, but I'm not sure what the best way of doing it is.  There's 
>limited disk space -- 1 300M drive with each workstation, maybe actually
>only a 100M with the 3100 -- so I was thinking of this:
>
>letting the 5000 be the NFS server, and exporting the whole /usr
>partition to the 3100.  This would mean that the 3100 would get most of
>its utility programs from the remote drive, but would execute locally.
>So the question is: can programs that are compiled on the 5000 be run
>on the 3100?

Yes.  Though the two should be running the same version of ULTRIX.

I'm lead to believe people have gotten this to work.  It isn't the
supported way to run a semi-diskless workstation, but it should work.
You'll probably need to rearrange some things between /etc/rc and
/etc/rc.local to make sure NFS will run early enough.  Of course
anything really on /usr that is needed before NFS is started and
the file system mounted will have to juggled.

In the typical default configurations most of /usr is read-only,
but the stuff in /usr/field and /usr/var is read-write.  /usr/field
needing to be read/write is a bug that has never been fixed, though
I've reported it a couple of times.  /usr/var is all the read/write
stuff on the traditional /usr.  The reason for putting them down a
file system level is that they can be moved to /var and be a separate
file system (for that matter so could /usr/var, but the preferred way
seems to be /var).

You'll notice that /usr/spool, /usr/adm, /usr/tmp (and others) are
symbolic links to the "var" versions.  You would want the server
setup so that its /var and the clients /var are different.  This
may require a 2nd disk on the 5000 just to get an extra file
system and at that point you'll find high capacity disks pretty
cheap.

>
>Also, I'm a little new to NFS, so is exporting the whole /usr partition
>a good idea?  If it isn't, what's a good way of hooking up the two 
>machines?

Using the Diskless Management Services is supported and well documented.
It is also uses more space and is setup for having many clients.

