Various people complained about companies 'hobbling' products.  I hope that
people won't mind if I re-post the following story.

	Mark

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Once upon a time there lived a great artisan whose fame had spread far and
wide.  One day he was summoned to the royal palace.  The King said to the
artisan

	I hear that your work is the finest in all my kingdom and so
	I wish to commission a hundred widgets for my palace.  Each
	widget must be coloured in the finest grey to match the stone
	of the palace walls.  I can pay ten kopecs for each widget.

The artisan considered this and replied

	Although I am honoured by your request it would cost me two
	thousand kopecs to develop the process to a standard worthy
	of displaying in your palace.  You are offering me a thousand
	kopecs which, while generous in the extreme, would leave me
	another thousand kopecs out of pocket; I cannot afford to
	accept your commission.

On his journey home he was recognised by the mayor of a small town who
also offered him a commission

	Our town is in need of three hundred widgets and we can afford
	to pay you five kopecs for each widget.

But the artisan had to turn down this commission also

	It would cost me two thousand kopecs to develop the process
	and you are offering only one and a half thousand kopecs so
	I would be five hundred kopecs out of pocket.

But the mayor was a wise man and replied

	I have heard of the offer that you received from the King.  If you
	were to sell widgets to both the King and to us then you could make a
	profit.  However, you are worried that we might resell some of our
	widgets to the King undercutting you.

	The solution is simple: I can sell you a bucket of red paint for
	one hundred kopecs and your problem will be solved.

The artisan still did not understand and so the mayor explained

	It costs you two thousand kopecs to develop the process.
	It costs you one hundred kopecs to buy the paint.  You
	make four hundred grey widgets and then dunk three hundred
	widgets in the paint at a total cost of two thousand one
	hundred kopecs.

	You sell three hundred red widgets to the town at five kopecs
	each making a total of 1500 kopecs.

	You sell one hundred widgets to the King (who will not buy our
	red widgets) at ten kopecs each; this makes a total of one thousand
	kopecs.

	Your profit is then 1500+1000-2100 = 400 kopecs.

		The End


If you think that it cannot be justified to `damage' a product in order
to reduce its value - for example you think that everybody should be sold
the better product at a lower price - then consider the following.

The town's people could not afford more than five kopecs (high price
sensitivity of demand).  If the artisan sold grey widgets to everybody
he would make 400*5 = 2000 kopecs but this is what it cost him to make
and so he makes no profit.  If he makes no profit then nobody gets any
widgets.


