In article <CCyyHr.Eq@nimno.wpa.com>, john@wpa.com (John Bartley) writes...
>I have about 6 months experience working with VMS, none with Open VMS and  
>none with HP-UX. I'm on a committee that is considering two competing  
>proposals from software vendors and am looking for outside viewpoints on  
>the major issues in the choice of moves. The committee has currently  
>favors the vendor that is proposing a package that runs under HP-UX.  
>However, the company so far has purchased only VAXes, and the    
>non-technical managers want to know why it would be advisable or at least  
>acceptable to bring in an HP box running Unix when everything is currently  
>VMS.
> 
>We can't stay VMS though, since the other vendor would require a move to  
>Open VMS.
> 

Sorry, your already running OpenVMS.  Digital says so.  The change from VMS to
OpenVMS [one more time folks] was just a rename by someone who thought it would
sell better.  It was inspired soley by the shipment of the POSIX interface for
VMS.

>Some topics that I'd like to hear other opinions on:    :-)
> 
>1) can an HP system (probably an HP 9000/720) peacefully coexist with the  
>VAX/VMS combination currently in place?
> 

Ours coexist.  Peacefully?  Not if you expect all the features of VMS.  We're
using DEC's UCX to establish TCP/IP communications from VMS to HP-UX, but
there are DECnet packages available for alomost anything, including HP-UX.

>2) what are the biggest advantages/disadvantages to switching to OpenVMS?
> 

Advantage:
1 DEC already did it for you.  Without touching a single byte of the software.
2 Critical operations like BACKUP work
3 User freindly operations like queuing to the next available idle printer of
  a given type work.
4 System managers don't have to write there own code to do routine operations
  like print to the same vendor's printers

Disadvantage:
Really, after over a year of both, the only disadvantage is dealling with
people who have heard the UNIX hype and believe it.  Porting applications
between UNIX's is a little easier than between non-UNIX's if you interface to
the operating system a lot.  I have found, though, that my VAX MACRO is
typically 100% portable to every VMS platform I've had my hands on (including
Alpha).

>3) what are the biggest advantages/disadvantages to switching to HP-UX?
> 

See above, reversed.  In particular we were displeased with being unable to
recover files which HP-UX's "fbackup" had convinced us it saved.  A few of our
programmers were upset when they lost hours of editing work when our
network went down (HP's fancy editor shipping with SoftBench neither journals
nor automaitcally saves).

>4) (For those with dual experience...) is there any significant difference  
>in the system administration time, effort, or cost between the two?
> 

I have administered a cluster of up to 28 VMS systems alone, part time.
I have one administrator each per flavor of UNIX, typically with only
3 or 4 sytems each.

>5) Which OS is more likely to be thriving 5 years from now? Why?
> 

WNT, maybe, my crystal ball isn't perfect, but I expect to see more
VMS, more UNIX, more DOS, and more WNT.  Some RSX, too.

>6) Does a change to OpenVMS also imply an upgrade to an Alpha processor,  
>or is that part optional? Are there any required hardware upgrades to move  
>from VMS to OpenVMS?
> 

OpenVMS VAX is what you are running on your VAX today.  OpenVMS AXP runs on
Alphas.  Your choice based on peripheral support, CPU power requirements, and
such.

>7) Finally, given that Oracle would be the DBMS used on either system,  
>which platform would offer the best combination of price/performance and  
>ease of use/administration?
> 

Oracle is supported on HP-UX, OpenVMS VAX, OpenVMS AXP, ULTRIX (for both VAX
and MIPS platforms), OSF-1 (currently the only UNIX shipping for the Alpha),
and just about any other popular platform you can think of.  Any RDMS can be a
CPU hog, if your sticking with VMS, you may be best of with an Alpha.  That
doesn't mean a VAX can't handle your situation, it depends on your
application's needs.

>Any "war stories," benchmarks, opinions, studies, etc. that relate to  
>these or other topics you believe are important would be most welcome.
> 

I'm sure there are many more.

