In article <26hj0r$pee@urmel.informatik.rwth-aachen.de>, marten@ilt.fhg.de (Lutz Marten) writes:
>
>Hello out there !
>I just received an brand new DSP5350 disk (5,25", 3570 megabytes)
>and found out that there is no partition table on it. (I'll tried
>chpt -q /dev/rrzXa ).
>Can anyone tell me the right partition table data for the DSP5350 ?
>Thanks !

The disk is probably recognized by ULTRIX as an RZxx.  This means it
will probably use the default partition table of:

	A =  32,768 sectors
	B = 131,072 sectors
	C = Entire disk
	G = Remainder of disk after A + B.

In the case of the DSP5350 the device is larger than the 2 GB device
limit on ULTRIX (4,194,303 sectors actually).  Even with over 100 MB
removed for the A and B partitions the default G is still too big to
be useful.  Meaning you want to put a partition table on the disk and
use chpt(8) to change it:

1.  Create a file system on A.  Doesn't matter much if you plan to
    reuse the file system later, but the goal is to get a partition
    table on the disk:

	# newfs /dev/rrz#a /dev/rrz#a

    By using the device name twice you give ULTRIX the oppurtunity to
    get the device geometry (or a suitable lie, this disk is banded)
    using DEVGETGEOM.

2.  Use chpt(8) to season to taste, keeping in mind the 2 GB device
    limit.  Other limits to consider are that the file system code
    likes working 8 KB blocks and nice multples of cylinder group
    sizes.  You may want to plan the partition sizes to reduce the
    wasted space for a 2 GB partition.
>
