  The problem is that vendors don't follow this analogy. Once the widgets
were sold, the artisan didn't care if the customers painted them - he had
received the fair price he asked for and (presumably) lived happily ever
after.

  In some cases I've seen, the computer vendor has gone ballistic. I can
understand this in the case of rented/leased/etc. equipment, but once a
customer purchases a system outright, it should be theirs to do with as
they please. The vendor can say "running at that speed is unsupported and
we can't support you", or "running the system as a higher model requires
a more expensive license", but "We will sue you for fraud" isn't accept-
able. Yet this is exactly what Basic-Four (MAI) did to customers who
found that their "25Mb" disk drives were actually 80Mb units and that the
CPU had 256Kb of memory, not 128Kb.

  Also, using your analogy, how much is the vendor's time worth? Why is
it practical for the vendor to incur a loss by spending money engineering
a slowed-down system when it isn't practical to lose the same or less
money shipping the full-capacity system?

  Likewise, since the vendor has no increased costs for the faster system,
(in the case of Basic-Four, they bought Calcomp "Trident" T-80 drives at
one price, whether they sold them as 80Mb or 25Mb units), why can't the
vendor sell the complete systems at the lower price?

  I'm not saying they have to - I just find such stunts distasteful. As
long as a vendor doesn't try to prevent folks from changing owned systems,
I don't think they should be forced to do anything special. However, they
might want to consider that things like this disappoint both their own
engineers and customers.

  In DEC's favor, they will sell the crystals and ROMs to upgrade various
CPU's as "spare parts". If a customer wants to risk wiping out a $10,000
board, they seem to feel it's the customer's right. I once upgraded a
PDP-11/73 CPU to an /83 by buying the faster CPU and FPA from DEC. It ran
well for several years and then failed in an unrelated manner. I returned
the board to DEC for repair, with a note that clearly stated that the board
had been sped up by installing genuine DEC parts, and if they didn't want
to touch it, I'd understand. I got a note back with my new module which
said: 1) Nice job on the upgrade 2) We discard all the parts but the CPU
and FPA chips, so we don't mind. Nice folks.

