I have two RD53's that came down with this disease, apparently during a long
period of disuse.  Swapping the logic board for one from a good RD53 didn't
help.  The interesting thing is that after first spin-up they'll get several 
minutes into the formatting process before the first spin-down, then they start
cycling.  

I took the cover off of one of the drive's HDAs (you know, undo all the
fasteners that are under the "warranty void if tampered with" seals) to expose
the platter and access arm.  I then added a couple of rubber feet to the, er,
"front" of the casting... the surface where the front panel would be if it had
one... and, placed on these feet on a shelf with the open HDA facing outward,
it makes a very nice "topical" bookend (to keep my VMS V6 manuals from flopping
over).  

