In article <CCyyHr.Eq@nimno.wpa.com>, john@wpa.com (John Bartley) writes:
>					The committee has currently  
> favors the vendor that is proposing a package that runs under HP-UX.  
> However, the company so far has purchased only VAXes, and the    
> non-technical managers want to know why it would be advisable or at least  
> acceptable to bring in an HP box running Unix when everything is currently  
> VMS.

If your organisation has a lot of VMS experience it seems like madness to
suddenly change to HP-UX. I wouldn't say there is much of a difference
between the two systems, but if you have a systems team who are used
to VMS and have a VMS environment setup then the most obvious would
seem to be to stick to VMS. If you were to setup a new system from scratch,
well depending on other requirements, HP-UX could be interesting.

> 4) (For those with dual experience...) is there any significant difference  
> in the system administration time, effort, or cost between the two?

Does that include Sendmail configuration? :-)

You usually have to get down to a lot of details when dealing with Unix,
but then again Unix people usually like that sort of stuff. VMS offers
a lot more consistent interface to SysAdm functions, and has generally
a more "mainframe" feel over it. This also goes for available hardware
configurations, cluster facilities, built-in security features, and
not least, the documentation.

If you have a quite complex environment with lots of different machines
that you need to interconnect (PCs, Suns, HPs, etc, etc) you'll probably
find that a Unix system is a lot more flexible. Also, if you're in an
engineering environment Unix is an excellent choice since it offers
a lot of flexibility. Sometimes more flexibility than stability, mind
you.

> 7) Finally, given that Oracle would be the DBMS used on either system,  
> which platform would offer the best combination of price/performance and  
> ease of use/administration?

From what I have gathered, Oracle is bigger on Unix systems than on VMS
so they are probably going to put more development efforts on their
Unix versions. If this was my only concern I would go for HP-UX rather
than VMS.

Anders.


