|>>1) can an HP system (probably an HP 9000/720) peacefully coexist with the  
|>>VAX/VMS combination currently in place?

Yes, especially if you have multinet tcp/ip running on the vaxen.  This
will allow them to 
share file systems, but not any of the real administration such as
passwords.  

|>>4) (For those with dual experience...) is there any significant difference  
|>>in the system administration time, effort, or cost between the two?

This is subjective, but I believe so.  VMS will protect the system
manager from many kinds
of mistakes while UNIX is unforgiving and requires tighter controls on
passwords and 
privlidges.  VMS lets me give people just enough privlidge to do their
work while 
UNIX really has only regular and root privlidges.  For this reason, UNIX
requires more
"people" control to keep things running smoothly, while VMS tends to
take care of itself.

|>>5) Which OS is more likely to be thriving 5 years from now? Why?

Recent studies suggest that the VMS world will shrink by about 25% while
the UNIX world is expected to grow more than 250% over the next 5 years.
I think these predictions were from IDC, a market research firm and published
around January of 1993 in Unix World magazine.

