In the three years since I last purchased a workstation, I have
completely lost touch with what's out there and now need to start
fresh.  My purpose in posting is to solicit up-to-date information
from satisfied/dissatisfied customers concerning competing vendors'
offerings, assuming there isn't already a FAQ with the kind of info I
need (any pointers?).  Since I assume the opinions that come in could
be of interest to lots of other people, I will gladly post a summary
of e-mail responses.

First let me describe what I'm generally looking for (probably in the
under-$50K range for the total package, including necessary "options"
such as thickwire ethernet and SCSI controller).

Hardware:
	High floating-point performance CPU
	efficient 8+-bit 2-D vector and raster graphics capability
	128-256 MB RAM; 

Base Software:
	UNIX-based operating system
	X11 server and libraries (R5, if possible)
	ANSI C compiler
	Fortran-77 compiler
	GKS graphics library

Purpose: Floating-point- and graphics-intensive scientific computing

Now let me describe my personal criteria for selecting a system (and
note that many of these are issues which vendors never even touch in
their promotional literature).


Turn-ons:

	Trouble-free installation and configuration

	Stable, relatively bug-free base operating system

	Reasonably efficient, bug-free X-windows operations

	Reasonably efficient, bug-free GKS library available, either
		from the vendor or from 3d party

	Ability to support 3-5 simultaneous users without "excessive"
		performance degradation
	
	Standards-conforming and relatively bug-free C and FORTRAN
		compilers (e.g., can I easily build various 3d party
		freeware/shareware applications on my machine without running
		into problems with weird compiler, linker,or runtime errors?)

	Excellent documentation, particularly for systems-admin stuff
		(i.e., does the vendor realize that not all workstation
		owners have paid UNIX gurus to look after the
		computer's care and feeding?)

	Excellent number-crunching performance

	Shared libraries, if possible

	Availability of useful BSD UNIX extensions and utilities (I
	understand System V is all that's being offered anymore, even
	by Sun)

	Trouble-free use of 3d party peripherals, including as system
	disks

	Good customer support and a generous warranty period

	Oh yeah, and I guess a good price/performance ratio would also be nice.

Turn-offs:	

	A system  which crashes (or goes into a pathological state
	requiring reboot) periodically for no legitimate reason

	A vendor who supplies docs that are incomplete, poorly
	written, and/or require a CS degree to understand

	A system which boasts excellent raw hardware performance on
	paper but whose vendor-supplied software (e.g., compilers,
	graphics libraries) contains bugs which make it
	difficult or impossible to do the work for which the system
	was purchased

	The vendor who supplies the buggy/poorly engineered software and
	then promises that "it will be fixed in the next release"

	The vendor who not only says that the bugs that cripple your
	application will be fixed only in the next release, BUT ALSO that you
	can't have the next release unless you pay through the nose
	for it (either as an outright purchase or by way of a
	maintenance contract), even though you ALREADY paid through
	the nose for the version that didn't work properly (imagine what would
	happen if Mercedes dealers took this approach!) 

	The vendor who releases software "upgrades"
	which don't correct the known bugs of earlier releases

	A vendor who pretends as though the only peripherals (e.g,.
	disks) you could ever rationally consider hanging on your
	machine are those of the vendor.

Based on what little I know right now, the systems that might fulfill
my most basic hardware needs at the moment include IBM RS6000s, DEC
Alphas, HPs, Suns, and SGIs (models?).  So would anyone care to offer
opinions, based on personal experience where possible, as to how these
machines/vendors stack up on any or all of the above points (or any
others you can think of)?  Please e-mail your responses and I will
summarize as best I can.




