In article <CCqEsu.JAn@news.iastate.edu>, john@iastate.edu (John Hascall) writes:
>Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
>From: john@iastate.edu (John Hascall)
>Subject: delay_wbuffers (was Re: Our DEC Alpha is the slowest on this code ....)
>
>alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com (Alan Rollow) writes:
>
>  [...previous poster complains about poor random write performance...]
>
>} [ And then I explain delay_wbuffers... ]

>   I tried this (setting delay_wbuffers=1) and then ran the
>   "iozone" benchmark (basically a sequential write and read)
>   and the performance was shot to hell on that...
>   (details: PDS/25, U4.3a, RZ25, "tunefs -a 8")
>
>   So it looks to me like you can have either good random
>   I/O performance OR good sequential I/O performance...

That may not be far from the truth.  Sequential writes, larger
than the cache will alternate between dirtying the cache and
then flushing it either as the result of a sync(2) or having to
flush dirty buffers to get clean buffers.  Once enough of the
cache is dirty, the application is going to be waiting for I/Os
to complete, before it can dirty the buffer with new data.

With delay_wbuffers clear and doing sequential writes, the write
is started soon after being copied into a buffer.  The result is
that the data is being steadily written and as each complete that
buffer is clean for later use.

Like most better I/O benchmarks iozone is probably tuned or recommends
doing sequential I/O larger than the cache.  For files smaller than
the cache, setting delay_wbuffer can make writing appear to be very
fast.

Also, note that the conditions that cause the asynchronous write of
a dirty buffer are different for ULTRIX and OSF/1.  By chosing the
right block size you can still get delayed I/O with delay_wbuffers
clear.  The condition that triggers the async. write is when the
I/O ends on a file system block boundary.  This may have changed
a little in V4.3 with the addition of maxcontig support.  I don't
have the V4.3 source to see.  If you can arrange for the application
not to write the entire file system block on an "update" the write
will be delayed.

