We have a user who needs all the CPU speed he can get.  The codes are three-
dimensional fluid-flow calculations that do not vectorize well; thus Crays
are essentially out.  He has benchmarked his code against all the 'hot' boxes
to get his best bang for the buck.  Because the program does very little I/O,
the test only treats the speed of the CPU.  Some recent runs on the code
include:

Sun Sparc 2          312 sec.
IBM RS/6000-340       78 sec.
DEC 3000-300L         52 sec.
DEC 3000-300          36 sec.

BTW, the 300L is slightly faster than a Cray 2 on _THIS_ code.

I do not represent DEC; however, I happen to like VMS and am very glad to
see a reasonable price/performance from a DEC machine.  The price for the
300L with 2 GB disk, CD-ROM and 64 MB with a 3-usr license, Fortran and
TCP/IP (MultiNet) is only slightly over $11K.

