Like Ken Cowan, I'm somewhat biased, but I do work on Fortran, so I can
respond in a bit more detail.

I'm not sure what gave you the impression that the Fortran compiler "was in
bad shape" and that the performance was poor.  We've had many customers
express delight in both the ease of migration from VAX (usually just a
recompile is all that's needed) and with the performance (it is with the
Fortran compiler that we get the industry-leading SPECmark ratings.)  Since
the initial release last fall, the Fortran compiler has gotten even better -
V6.1 is now out along with OpenVMS AXP V1.5; performance is up and VAX
compatibility is even better.

|>(Personally I would try to jump to OSF/1.  Isn't the Fortran compiler on Alpha/OSF/1
|>very similar to the VMS version?)
|>

Are you asking if the DEC OSF/1 AXP compiler is similar to the OpenVMS AXP
version?  If so, the answer is yes, they are almost identical.  The AXP
compilers "front end" was derived from that of the VAX FORTRAN compiler.
We've tried as best as we can to make the VAX and AXP compilers as functionally
similar as possible.  As of V6.1 of both compilers (The V6.1 VAX compiler is
due to be released next month), the major remaining differences are:

	AXP does not support the RAD50 functions and subroutines nor the
	RAD50 syntax for constants in DATA statements.  (But I have developed
	a set of the RAD50 routines written in Fortran which I will be glad
	to send to anyone who writes me.)

	AXP does not support the PDP-11 I/O routines.

	AXP does not yet support a REAL*16 datatype - that will come next
	year.

	AXP does not yet support the DICTIONARY statement.

	If your application passes quoted character literals as arguments
	to routines which expect them as non-CHARACTER types, you must
	either compile the caller and callee together, or use the %REF
	mechanism modifier function when passing the argument.  (We do
	intend to make this work as transparently as it did on VAX, but
	it will be a while.)

That's pretty much it.

If you have specific concerns about DEC Fortran for AXP, please feel free
to write me.
--

