In article <1D772AE5TS@TOSCHIBO.zer.sub.org>, T_SCHILLER@TOSCHIBO.swb.de writes:
> I am looking for information about the VAX 8300.
> 
> it got four Harddrives: 2 x RZ82 + 2 x RZ81.
> 
> I'm interessted in the DCSI-BUS of these Harddrive.
> Is it a SCSI-Bus, where DEC changed the name ( S(mall)CSI to D(EC)CSI) ?
> If not:
>   Is there any possibility to connect these harddrives to an IBM-clone?

  I think you've been misinformed. The disk drives are most likely RA81's
and RA82's (RA is the only DEC drive series with an 81 and 82 model). These
are called "SDI interface", which is a proprietary-to-DEC interface. The
controller is probably a KDB50 4-port BI to SDI adapter.

  DEC's "enhanced SCSI" (their term, not mine) is called DSSI (Digital Stor-
age Subsystem Interconnect, if I remember right). It uses an interface which
is electrically similar to SCSI with a different command set.

  The VAX 8300 is a dual-processor system which (on a good day) is twice as
fast as a VAX 780, or slightly better than two times a MicroVAX II. It uses
BI peripheral controllers, which are somewhat hard to come by. BI was DEC's
experiment in "closing" the system bus so they could have all of the revenue.
I believe it's generally agreed that it didn't work (to keep 3rd parties
out, that it. It functioned passably in the technical sense.).

