We still have (i.e., "have" in the *present* tense) a few of these
boat anchors in various configurations. So I can vouch that Mr.
Pollack's post is correct, at least as far as the number of CPUs goes.
The VUP ratings need a few caveats, however.
  As many people are aware, and as was pointed out in a previous post,
the 82xx/83xx was a BI bus system. What many people aren't aware is
that the BI bus on these systems wasn't just the periperal
interconnect bus in the same manner as it was used in the higher
performance VAX 8000 series systems. On the 82xx/83xx, the BI bus was
also used as the system bus.  CPUs, memory, and peripheral controllers
were all attached to the same BI bus. As a result, the machine was a
somewhat less than stellar performer. 
  Our example of this system freqeuently spent 100% or more (of a
total 200%) of its CPU time in executive mode, kernel mode or on the
interupt stack, as reported by VMS monitor. Granted the performance
wasn't always that bad, but for every time I saw 180-200% user mode, I
also recall seeing less than 10% total user mode on a busy system
twice as often. So to call an 8350 a 2.7 VUP machine is a just wee bit
generous.
  Also amusing is the less well known fact that if you put a third CPU
board into an 8350, it would boot up as an 8370. Naturally, that was
an unsupported configuration and not well publicised.

